              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">2-3 yrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Floor recoat, semi-transparent</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">1 yr</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Floor recoat, translucent in sun</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Those numbers are typical ranges from labels and owner experience, not guarantees. A south-facing deck in a hot climate will need attention sooner than a shaded one, and the quality of the prep affects longevity more than the price per gallon.
          </p>

          <p>
            Maintenance is where oil stains shine. Each spring, wash the deck and check the high-wear areas. When water stops beading or the color fades, clean, brighten and apply one thin coat of the same product. Keep a sealed quart of your color for touch-ups, and you will rarely face a full strip.
          </p>

          <p>
            A rough cost comparison helps put premium pricing in perspective. If a budget oil costs $35 a gallon and needs a floor recoat every year, while a premium oil costs $70 and goes three years, the premium option uses fewer gallons, fewer weekends and fewer cleaning cycles over the same period. The cheaper can only wins when the deck is small, shaded or likely to be replaced soon. For decks finished with a clear product instead, our <a href="/best-deck-sealant/">deck sealant</a> guide covers those options.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving Excess Oil on the Surface</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Oil that the wood cannot absorb stays soft and tacky, picks up dirt and footprints and can take weeks to firm up. Apply thin coats, back-brush and wipe off anything still shiny after the dwell time on the label. A thin coat that soaks in always outperforms a thick one that sits on top.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Balling Up Oily Rags</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Rags soaked with linseed or other drying oils can self-heat and ignite when piled or bagged. Lay them flat outdoors to dry or seal them in a water-filled metal can with a tight lid. This is a common, preventable cause of fires, and it applies to every oil stain on this list.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Staining Before a Cool, Damp Spell</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Oil stains cure slowly in cold and humid weather. If temperatures drop or dew forms before the stain dries, it can stay sticky or cloud. Check the forecast for two dry days within the temperature range on the label, and start early so the stain has warm hours to dry.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Stir</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Pigment settles to the bottom of a can of oil stain quickly. Without thorough stirring, the first part of the deck gets thin, light color and the last part gets heavy pigment. Stir well before starting and every so often while working, and mix multiple gallons together for a consistent color.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the Wrong Formula for Your State</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many oil stains come in versions with different VOC limits. Ordering a high-VOC formula into a regulated area is not allowed, and online orders are often cancelled. Check the VOC content on the label and buy the compliant version for your area; most perform very close to the originals.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which qualities separate a good oil stain from a poor one?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A good oil stain penetrates deeply rather than forming a film, carries enough pigment to block UV for two years or more on a sunny floor, includes mildewcides if your deck is shaded, and wears by fading rather than peeling. It should also be legal in your state and come with clear instructions on coats and dwell time.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do oil-based stains last longer than water-based ones?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Often, but not always. Oil stains usually wear more gracefully because they fade instead of peeling, which makes them seem longer-lasting and easier to maintain. Good water-based stains can hold color as long or longer. Prep quality, sun exposure and foot traffic usually matter more than the chemistry alone, so choose on handling and maintenance as much as on claimed lifespan.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which oil stain suits pressure-treated pine?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Penetrating semi-transparent oils such as Armstrong Clark, TWP and Ready Seal suit dry pressure-treated pine well. Choose a warm cedar or brown tone to offset any green tint, wait until the boards pass the water-drop test, and apply thin coats. Avoid staining treated lumber that is still wet from the treatment process.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How is a penetrating stain different from a film-forming one?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A penetrating stain soaks into the wood and protects from within, so it wears by fading and recoats without stripping. A film-forming stain, such as most solid stains and paints, sits on top as a layer. Films can look uniform and protect well at first, but they crack and peel when they fail.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often does an oil-stained deck need a fresh coat?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Plan on recoating the floor about every two to three years with a semi-transparent oil, and roughly every year with a translucent oil on a sunny deck. Railings usually last longer. Wash the deck each spring and recoat when water no longer beads or the color has noticeably faded in traffic areas.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can oil stain go over an old water-based finish?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if the old finish is almost completely gone. Oil cannot penetrate through an intact water-based film, so it will sit on top and fail. Strip the old coating or let it weather away, brighten the wood, check that water soaks in evenly across every board, then apply the oil stain in thin coats.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are oil stains safe to use near the house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, when you follow the label. Work outdoors with good airflow, keep nearby windows closed while it dries, keep children and pets away until dry, and avoid open flames. Handle oily rags carefully because they can self-heat and ignite, and dispose of leftovers and used solvent at a household hazardous waste site.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

